<TMU|<tuple|1.1.0|2025.0.15>>

<style|<tuple|generic|chinese|number-europe>>

<\body>
  In neural network, we are estimating the "activate process" of neuron. When receiving the signal, the state becomes 1, while not is 0. So the final vector we get for n receivers is a one-hot vector.

  If we want to estimate this process in a smooth way, in another word, we want to do derivative on this process, this is the optimization problem we are facing,choosing a good probability distribution to maximum:

  <\equation>
    w<rsup|\<ast\>>=<below|argmax|w\<in\>\<Delta\><rsup|K>><around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|w,y|\<rangle\>>|}>,
  </equation>

  where <math|y> are the scores of neurons.

  \;

  For changing this to a convex problem since we want to do GD, we can add a regularizer:

  <\equation>
    w<rsup|\<ast\>>=<below|argmax|w\<in\>\<Delta\><rsup|K>><around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|w,y|\<rangle\>>-\<beta\><below|<big|sum>|i\<in\><around*|[|K|]>>w<rsub|i>ln<around*|(|w<rsub|i>|)>|}>,
  </equation>

  we can solve by KKT:

  <\equation>
    w<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>=<frac|exp<around*|(|<frac|y<rsub|i>|\<beta\>>|)>|<below|<big|sum>|j\<in\><around*|[|K|]>>exp<around*|(|<frac|y<rsub|j>|\<beta\>>|)>>=<frac|1|<below|<big|sum>|j\<in\><around*|[|K|]>>exp<around*|(|<frac|y<rsub|j>-y<rsub|i>|\<beta\>>|)>>.
  </equation>

  <math|\<beta\>\<rightarrow\>0,w<rsup|\<ast\>>\<rightarrow\>e<rsub|i<rsup|\<ast\>>>.>

  So adding regularizer is just a way to proceed this problem to continuity approximation by soft-max.

  For 2-classification problem, Softmax<math|<around*|(|x|)>=<frac|e<rsup|x<rsub|1>>|e<rsup|x<rsub|1>>+e<rsup|x<rsub|2>>>=<frac|1|1+exp<around*|(|x<rsub|2>-x<rsub|1>|)>>=\<sigma\><around*|(|x<rsub|2>-x<rsub|1>|)>.>

  Then we talk about ReLU, which may not so obvious to be like either sigmoid or softmax,

  but if you not only see, but observe, you will find:

  \;

  <\equation>
    ReLU<around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|{|0,x|}>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>+\<infty\>><frac|log<around*|(|e<rsup|kx>+1|)>|k>,
  </equation>

  <\equation>
    <around*|(|<frac|log<around*|(|e<rsup|kx>+1|)>|k>|)><rprime|'>=<frac|1|k>\<times\><frac|1|e<rsup|kx>+1>\<times\>e<rsup|kx>\<times\>k=<frac|1|1+e<rsup|-kx>>=sigmoid<around*|(|x|)>,
  </equation>

  where ReLU in this sense can be treated as the cumulation of the signal. We say cumulation is because here we have a integral to connect sigmoid and ReLU. Not with standing, if we take k to be 1, we get smoothed ReLU.

  \;

  \;

  \;

  \;

  \;
</body>

